{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f4cbfc58a683894a66b028420969bb75a35fe4acda1bd3dce0024062c11b2041", "sample_idx": 34, "model_name": "mistralai/mixtral-8x7b-instruct-v0.1", "content": " \"The following are the opening statements of a news article from the Guardian.\nClick here to view the print edition. The usual Sudoku regulations apply, with the exception that the figures in the cells enclosed by gray lines must total the amounts in the corner. No number can be duplicated inside each form developed by gray lines. Get next week's Observer Digital Edition to see the finished puzzle.\"\n\n\"Additionally, there are a few more regulations that are specific to this kind of Sudoku game. Each shape created by the gray lines, which function as cages, needs to have its own total in the corner. This means that the numbers in each shape must add up to the figure indicated in the cage's corner, and no number can be repeated within that shape. These regulations provide an extra layer of complexity to the game, making it more challenging and interesting for Sudoku enthusiasts.\"\n\n\"It is important to note that the objective of Sudoku, whether it is a standard Sudoku or this modified version, remains the same. The goal is to fill in the grid with numbers from 1 to 9, with each number appearing only once in each row, column, and 3x3 box. The addition of the gray lines and their corresponding corner totals simply adds another layer of strategy and complexity to the game.\"\n\n\"To solve this Sudoku puzzle, players must use a combination of logical reasoning and mathematical skills. They need to look for patterns and clues within the grid, such as the numbers that are already filled in and the totals in the corners of the gray lines. By analyzing this information, players can make educated guesses and eliminate possibilities, gradually filling in the grid until it is complete.\"\n\n\"Sudoku is a popular puzzle game that has captivated people all over the w